## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a dynamic ICA pipeline that handles changing source counts and moving sources in a real-world, noisy environment. It emphasizes adaptation mechanisms, cross-bin alignment strategies, and practical latency constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Adaptive whitening with forgetting factors for non-stationary noise
- Dynamic demixer dimension that expands/contracts with N
- Criteria for per-bin versus joint-diagonalization approaches
- Cross-bin permutation resolution via spectral envelopes and coarse DOA cues
- Real-time on-device evaluation using ground-truth speaker positions and VAD

## Code Example
```javascript
function onlineICAFrame(frame, state) {
  // 1) Online whitening with forgetting factor
  const whitenState = updateWhitening(frame, state.whiten, state.forgettingFactor);
  const whitened = applyWhitening(frame, whitenState.mean, whitenState.eigen);

  // 2) Estimate source count and adapt demixer dimension
  const estimatedN = estimateSourceCount(whitened, state.threshold);
  const demixer = adaptDemixer(state.demixer, estimatedN, state.stepSize);

  // 3) Demixing via online ICA (e.g., Natural Gradient)
  const separated = demix(whitened, demixer);

  // 4) Permutation alignment via envelope continuity + coarse DOA
  const doas = computeCoarseDOA(separated, state.micPositions);
  const aligned = alignPermutations(separated, state.envelopeRefs, doas);

  return { output: aligned, state: { ...state, demixer, whitenState, estimatedN, doas } };
}

function adaptDemixer(demixer, newN, stepSize) {
  if (newN > demixer.N) return expandDemixer(demixer, newN - demixer.N, stepSize);
  if (newN < demixer.N) return contractDemixer(demixer, demixer.N - newN);
  return demixer;
}
```

